  "description": "An embeddable holographic SQL database for .NET: transactional (ACID with a write-ahead log), columnar storage with compiled predicates, hash joins, and similarity search in SQL (NEAREST). Integer COUNT, SUM, MIN and MAX read maintained exact accumulators, so whole-table and single-column grouped aggregates do not scan; SUM is exact past 64 bits and throws only if the true total leaves Int64. SQL NULL on columns declared NULL (three-valued logic, IS [NOT] NULL, aggregates that skip NULLs; SUM, AVG, MIN and MAX of no rows are NULL), UPDATE and DELETE, INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE, and schema evolution via CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS / ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN. An optional durable larger-than-memory tier: paged tables on a buffer pool and write-ahead log (no memory-mapped files), cold pages demand-paged from disk, a CRC-32C on every data page, UPDATE and DELETE by key or index, and statements larger than the buffer pool. Exact summaries and indexes that are derived from the columns and can always be rebuilt from them: an optional Pleroma aggregate plane (small-domain columns; answers WHERE aggregates, multi-column GROUP BY, COUNT(DISTINCT), MIN/MAX and equi-join COUNT/SUM, declining to the engine outside its caps), a sorted index and a keyed summary with pre-grouped join totals for paged tables, declared with CREATE INDEX ... USING SUMMARY | SORTED | KEYED. Paged tables save them beside the data and trust them only for the exact table state they were saved from, so a database restored from a backup answers from them at once. By default the engine also builds them itself when repeated scans have paid for the build, and drops them when the workload moves on (SET summaries / SET indexes; EXPLAIN and SHOW SUMMARIES say why). A Pleroma page store (exact Phasor Pleroma and sparse pages as durable, larger-than-memory data with concurrent readers, a crash-safe add journal, restore points, an event journal, check-prime verification and parity repair). Built for real-time metrics: live aggregate reads are served lock-free from a snapshot published after each write; ApplyChanges and INSERT ... ON CONFLICT apply a change feed of upserts and deletes as one atomic batch, across several tables in one call, and KeyDigest fingerprints an INT key set in one pass; summary tables (CREATE SUMMARY TABLE) keep no rows, only the exact plane, with Add / Remove / Replace batches applied exactly once by feed position. Parallel scans, group-by and top-K run as EvalApp pipelines; a per-query usage meter (rows and bytes processed) is available for billing. Integrates as an EvalApp extension with async execution.",
